.TH RULES 1 "2026-08-20" "rules 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rules \- print applicable repository instructions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rules
.RI [ DIR ]
.br
.B rules \-list
.RI [ DIR ]
.br
.B rules
.RB { help | version }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rules
prints the bounded repository instructions that apply at a directory.
The default directory is the current working directory.
.PP
The project root is the nearest ancestor containing a
.B .git
directory or regular
.B .git
file.
Rules walks from that root to the target directory.
At each level it reads
.B AGENTS.md
before
.BR CLAUDE.md .
Thus broader files precede narrower files.
.PP
Every applicable logical path is considered.
When multiple paths have the same fully resolved path, its body is written
only at the first path's position.
Later paths remain visible under
.B \-list
and are identified as aliases on standard error.
Distinct files are not deduplicated by content.
.PP
Instruction text is standard output.
Root, source paths, resolved symlink targets, byte counts, and errors are
standard error.
Rules reads and validates the complete set before writing standard output.
It never truncates or emits a partial set after a refusal.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-list
Write one applicable logical path per line instead of instruction text.
The files and limits are still validated.
.TP
.BR \-h , " " \--help , " " help
Print a command summary.
.TP
.BR \-V , " " \--version , " " version
Print the Rules version.
.TP
.B \--
End option parsing, permitting a directory beginning with a hyphen.
.SH LIMITS
The unique applicable fully resolved file bodies may contain at most 32 KiB in
total, so one file is subject to the same bound.
The limit is fixed and has no configuration or environment override.
An empty set is valid and writes empty standard output.
.SH SYMLINKS
The target directory is canonicalized before root discovery.
An instruction symlink is fully resolved and accepted only when its target
remains inside the canonical project root.
The logical path is printed by
.BR \-list ;
standard error shows the resolved path.
A common
.B CLAUDE.md
link to
.B AGENTS.md
therefore keeps both logical names without repeating the body.
A symbolic
.B .git
entry is not a root marker.
.SH COMPOSITION
Rules does not modify a model prompt or invoke another program.
Composition is explicit in the shell, for example:
.PP
.nf
ASK_SYSTEM="$(ask system; rules)" \
  ply -sh -check 'go test ./...' 'fix it'
.fi
.PP
In an unattended script, check each producer before starting the agent:
.PP
.nf
system=$(ask system) &&
workspace=$(rules) &&
ASK_SYSTEM="$system
$workspace" ply -sh -check 'go test ./...' 'fix it'
.fi
.PP
The separate assignments make a Rules exit of 1 or 2 stop the chain.
.PP
Repository instructions are guidance, not authorization.
Their prompt position must not grant secrets, network access, filesystem
access, approvals, or destructive capabilities; those remain runner and
operator decisions.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The complete set was printed, including an empty set.
.TP
.B 1
No project root was found, or an instruction was refused.
.TP
.B 2
The invocation was invalid, an I/O operation failed, or stdout failed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B AGENTS.md
First instruction name at each directory level.
.TP
.B CLAUDE.md
Second instruction name at each directory level.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ask (1),
.BR ply (1)
